\check Mid-sentence Capital
\submenu Phil_new
\script mid_sentence_capital.py
\description Flags a capital letter that appears mid-sentence: after a comma, semicolon, or another word rather than after sentence-final punctuation. Capitalized Biblical Terms renderings and words the project's wordlist always capitalizes can be ignored. Options let you set sentence-boundary characters, words/prefixes/suffixes to ignore, quote-opening handling, and character/paragraph styles to skip.
\books
\noText
\toList
\utf8

\optionName FinalPunctuation
\optionLocalizedName Sentence boundary characters
\optionDescription Characters that mark a sentence boundary. Capitalized words immediately following any of these are not flagged. Default covers . ! ? inverted sentence-opening punctuation is handled automatically.
\optionDefault .!?

\optionName WordsToIgnore
\optionLocalizedName Words to ignore
\optionDescription Comma-separated list of capitalized words that should not be flagged (e.g. Senor, Alfa, Espiritu). Use this for proper nouns not covered by TermRenderings.xml. This is also the base-word list that Word prefixes/suffixes to ignore build on: a listed word with one of those prefixes or suffixes attached is also not flagged.
\optionDefault

\optionName IgnoreBiblicalTerms
\optionLocalizedName Ignore capitalized Biblical Terms
\optionDescription Set to Yes to skip a capitalized word or phrase that is a rendering in the project's Biblical Terms (TermRenderings.xml), such as a proper name. Set to No to flag these too; prefixes/suffixes to ignore then build only on Words to ignore and the wordlist.
\optionDefault Yes

\optionName IgnoreWordlistCapitals
\optionLocalizedName Ignore capitalized words in the Wordlist
\optionDescription Set to Yes to skip a word that the project's Wordlist marks as always capitalized (a specific case in SpellingStatus.xml, e.g. Galilaaya). A listed word with one of the prefixes/suffixes to ignore attached is also skipped.
\optionDefault Yes

\optionName IgnoreCapAfterQuote
\optionLocalizedName Ignore capital after opening quote
\optionDescription Set to Yes to suppress errors where a capitalized word follows an opening quotation mark (e.g. direct speech after a comma). The characters treated as openers are configured by the Opening quote characters option below.
\optionDefault No

\optionName QuoteOpeningChars
\optionLocalizedName Opening quote characters
\optionDescription Characters treated as opening quotation marks when "Ignore capital after opening quote" is set to Yes. Each character in this string is an opener. Default covers left guillemet («), left double curly quote (“), em dash (—), and left single curly quote (‘). Clear this field to fall back to Unicode Pi-category detection.
\optionDefault «“—‘

\optionName IgnoreStyles
\optionLocalizedName Character styles to ignore
\optionDescription Comma-separated USFM character style names (no backslash) whose enclosed text is not checked for mid-sentence capitals and is excluded from end-of-verse detection. Use for paired styles like qs (Selah) or pn (proper names) that contain capitalized words that should not be flagged.
\optionDefault

\optionName IgnoreParaStyles
\optionLocalizedName Paragraph styles to ignore
\optionDescription Comma-separated USFM paragraph marker names (no backslash) whose text is treated as a sentence boundary and excluded from end-of-verse detection. Default covers: sp (speaker), r (parallel reference), ms/mr (major section), sr (section range). Section headings (s, s1, s2...) are suppressed automatically.
\optionDefault sp, r, ms, mr, sr

\optionName PrefixesToIgnore
\optionLocalizedName Word prefixes to ignore
\optionDescription Space-separated list of word prefixes (e.g. ra Ra). A capitalized word made of one of these plus either a word listed in Words to ignore, or a capitalized Biblical Terms rendering this check already recognizes from TermRenderings.xml (case-insensitive), will not be flagged. Has no effect on a word whose remainder after the prefix isn't recognized either way. An entry can also be a "/"-separated group of interchangeable variants (e.g. i/ɨ/ʉ) for languages where attaching the prefix changes the following letter(s) rather than just adding characters: if the word starts with one variant, each other variant in the group is tried in its place when reconstructing the word to check against Words to ignore / TermRenderings.xml.
\optionDefault

\optionName SuffixesToIgnore
\optionLocalizedName Word suffixes to ignore
\optionDescription Space-separated list of word suffixes (e.g. es am). A capitalized word made of one of these plus either a word listed in Words to ignore, or a capitalized Biblical Terms rendering this check already recognizes from TermRenderings.xml (case-insensitive), will not be flagged. Has no effect on a word whose remainder before the suffix isn't recognized either way. An entry can also be a "/"-separated group of interchangeable variants (e.g. ión/iones, or ɨ/ʉ) for languages where the stem's ending changes when a suffix attaches rather than the suffix simply being added on: if the word ends in one variant, each other variant in the group is tried in its place when reconstructing the word to check against Words to ignore / TermRenderings.xml.
\optionDefault

\optionName MidWordPunctuation
\optionLocalizedName Mid-word punctuation
\optionDescription Characters that may appear inside a word (e.g. - for hyphen, ' for apostrophe). Enter without spaces. Words containing these characters are treated as a single unit when checking for capitals.
\optionDefault -

